**The bug**: this treats "subkey has no expiry set" (`fields[6] == "" || "0"`) the same as "subkey is expired" — both just `continue` without hard-failing, as long as SOME other signing-capable subkey has a valid, current expiry. But I proved empirically with real gpg (cert-only primary key, an older signing subkey with a real 1-year expiry, a NEWER signing subkey created with NO expiry) that `gpg --detach-sign -u <identity>` selects the NEWER, NON-EXPIRING subkey to actually sign with — not the older, properly-expiring one. This means `CheckKeyExpiry` can report "pass" (because the older subkey satisfies "at least one currently valid") while `Sign`/`DetachSignBody` actually sign with the non-expiring subkey, completely bypassing the "non-expiring keys rejected" invariant (DES-020) — the original, most fundamental security property this whole feature exists to enforce.

The distinguishing fact that makes the two cases different: an EXPIRED subkey (one that WAS properly configured with a real expiry, and that date has passed) will NEVER be selected by gpg for a new signing operation — I proved this empirically too (gpg picks the valid one when one exists, and refuses to sign at all if none are valid). So leniency for "expired" is safe. But a NON-EXPIRING subkey (empty or "0" expiry field) is NOT expired — it's a live, selectable, currently-usable key from gpg's point of view, and gpg's subkey-selection algorithm can and does pick it over a properly-configured one. Leniency for "no expiry set" is NOT safe.

**The fix**: restore hard, immediate rejection for "signing-capable subkey has no expiry set at all" — no leniency, regardless of whether other subkeys are valid — while KEEPING the leniency only for "signing-capable subkey has an expiry set but it has passed":

